From b70f73a64ca491a05b6469ec3bf07957886f050d Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: genspark_dev <genspark_dev@genspark.ai>
Date: Thu, 24 Sep 2026 05:15:02 +0300
Subject: [PATCH] Ship A4 print layouts for the three management reports

Frappe renders a report layout in the browser, not on the server: the report's print dialog
returns the format's html and css through frappe.desk.query_report.get_print_format_data and
query_report.js::pdf_report injects them, so the template sees title, subtitle, filters, data,
columns, original_data, report - and may not call frappe.*, because there is no server-side render
for it to reach. The templates are therefore executed in the tests against real columns and rows,
which is what checks the total row, the alternating rows, the empty period and the dashes.

One A4 layout ships three times, one row per report: a coloured title band, a filter strip, a dark
teal table head, zebra rows, the total row highlighted, and a footer stating that the report is
computed under the printing user's permissions. Arabic and right-to-left, with the Arabic font
named so an English site does not fall back to a Latin default.

They are created by an installer rather than imported from the module folder, for the framework's
own reason: PrintFormat.before_save forces custom_format=1 and standard="No" on every report
format, because Frappe treats a report layout as a site customisation rather than a document an app
ships - and a row that is not standard=Yes is not re-imported on migrate. setup/report_print_formats.py
creates it through frappe.get_doc().insert(), exactly what Desk does when a Print Format is saved
by hand. It is idempotent, never overwrites an existing format because a site may have restyled it,
and provision.apply calls it so one command still prepares a site.

The metadata states custom_format=1 and standard="No" up front, instead of claiming "Yes" and
being rewritten on the first save.
